-- 04: Handlers, Handler Clauses, Guards, Destructuring (issue #4)
-- The reference flavour first, unchanged:

on handle {type: "invoice", amount: a} as inv where a > 1000
  send review with inv to approvals
end handle

on handle {type: "invoice"} as inv
  file inv
end handle

-- ?? `it` here means "the whole message argument". In HyperTalk `it` is the
--    result of the last `get`/`ask`/`answer`. We'd now have two meanings.
--    Options: keep `it` for the argument only when the head is a pattern;
--    or bind the whole with `as`:
on handle {type: "refund"} as refund
  send review with refund to approvals
  -- ?? `send refund to approvals` parses too, but it sends a message NAMED
  --    `refund` with no argument: the word after `send` is always the message.
end handle
-- ALT: on handle refund = {type: "refund"}    -- Elixir order; `=` reads as a test

-- Clauses are tried top to bottom; the first match runs.
on handle {type: "invoice", lines: []}
  -- ?? This clause can never run: the one above it (`{type: "invoice"}`) already
  --    matches. Lint: "unreachable clause".
end handle

-- A final catch-all. With no catch-all, an unmatched message follows the
-- Message Path (ADR 0004).
on handle other
  pass handle
  -- ALT: pass                             -- the message name is implied
end handle

-- Several arguments: each is its own pattern.
on move [x, y], speed where speed > 0 m/s
  put x & "," & y into position
end move

-- Kind tests go in the Guard (the grammar has no kind test inside a pattern)
on price {amount: a, currency: c} where a is a number and c is a text
  say a & " " & c
end price
-- ALT: on price {amount: a: number, currency: c: text}   -- too many colons
-- ALT: on price {amount: number a, currency: text c}

-- Quantity Kind in a Guard
on travel d where d is a length
  -- ?? `is a length` tests the Unit Kind. Is `length` a Unit Kind name or the
  --    `length` function? Probably need `is a distance`, or `has unit kind length`.
end travel

-- Pin: match against an existing variable instead of binding a new name.
-- [advanced]
on reply {id: ^pending, answer: a}
  put a into result
end reply
-- ALT: on reply {id: the value of pending, answer: a}
-- ALT: on reply {id: pending, answer: a} where id = pending   -- beginner-friendly, no pin
-- ?? Without the pin, `{id: pending}` BINDS `pending` (names in patterns always bind,
--    issue #4). That is the classic Elixir trap. The beginner lint should warn when
--    a pattern name shadows a Script Variable.

-- Nested Destructuring and lists
on order {customer: {name: n}, items: [first, ...rest]}
  say n & " ordered " & (the length of rest + 1) & " things"
end order

-- `match` is the same machinery inside a Handler
on classify v
  match v
    when [] then return "empty list"
    when [x] then return "one: " & x
    when [x, y, ...] then return "starts " & x & ", " & y
    when {kind: k} where k is not "secret" then return "a " & k
    when n where n is a number and n < 0 then return "negative"
    else return "something else"
  end match
end classify
-- ALT: `otherwise` instead of `else` in match

-- Guards are pure and total: an error in a Guard just skips the clause.
on score {points: p} where p as number > 10
  -- `p as number` failing (p = "lots") skips this clause rather than raising.
  -- ?? Is that surprising? It matches issue #4, but it hides typos.
end score
